@page "/razor-components"
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations

<PageTitle>Razor Components</PageTitle>

<h1>Razor components</h1>

<p class="lead">
    "Tags" in a <code>.razor</code> file are either ordinary HTML elements, built-in Blazor components,
    or your own components. This page shows how each works.
</p>

<hr/>

<h2>1. Any HTML tag is valid</h2>

<p>Razor is a superset of HTML. Every HTML element works exactly as you expect:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;div&gt;, &lt;span&gt;, &lt;p&gt;, &lt;h1&gt;, &lt;table&gt;, &lt;ul&gt;, &lt;input&gt;, &lt;select&gt;, &lt;form&gt;, ...</code></pre>

<p>The only additions are the <code>@@</code>-prefixed Razor attributes (<code>@@onclick</code>, <code>@@bind</code>, ...)
    and the component tags from sections 2-4 below.</p>

<h2>2. Built-in Blazor components</h2>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
    <tr>
        <th>Component</th>
        <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;PageTitle&gt;</code></td><td>Sets the browser tab title.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;HeadOutlet&gt;</code></td><td>Renders <code>PageTitle</code>/<code>HeadContent</code> (in <code>App.razor</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;NavLink&gt;</code></td><td>An <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> that knows when it matches the current route (adds <code>active</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;Router&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;RouteView&gt;</code></td><td>Route matching (see <code>Routes.razor</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;</code></td><td>Catches exceptions thrown by child content.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;Virtualize&gt;</code></td><td>Renders only the visible rows of a large list.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;EditForm&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;InputText&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;InputNumber&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ValidationMessage&gt;</code></td><td>Data-annotated form binding and validation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;FocusOnNavigate&gt;</code></td><td>Focuses an element after navigation (see <code>Routes.razor</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;AuthorizeView&gt;</code></td><td>Renders content conditionally by user (needs authentication configured).</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h3>NavLink example</h3>

<nav class="nav nav-pills mb-4">
    <NavLink class="nav-link" href="/" Match="NavLinkMatch.All">Home</NavLink>
    <NavLink class="nav-link" href="/counter">Counter</NavLink>
    <NavLink class="nav-link" href="/weather">Weather</NavLink>
    <NavLink class="nav-link" href="/razor-syntax">Razor Syntax</NavLink>
</nav>

<p>
    <code>Match="NavLinkMatch.All"</code> matches only the exact URL;
    the default (Prefix) matches the URL and everything under it.
</p>

<h2>3. Your own components: parameters</h2>

<p>The component below is defined in <code>Components/Shared/PersonCard.razor</code>.
    Values are passed through <code>[Parameter]</code> properties; the inner content is the special
    <code>ChildContent</code> parameter.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;PersonCard Name="Ada Lovelace" Age="36"&gt;
    Wrote the first algorithm intended for a machine.
&lt;/PersonCard&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="row g-3 mb-4">
    <div class="col-md-4">
        <PersonCard Name="Ada Lovelace" Age="36">Wrote the first algorithm for a machine.</PersonCard>
    </div>
    <div class="col-md-4">
        <PersonCard Name="Grace Hopper" Age="85">Invented the first compiler.</PersonCard>
    </div>
    <div class="col-md-4">
        <PersonCard Name="Alan Turing" Age="41">Developed the Turing machine.</PersonCard>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>4. Named render fragments (multi-section content)</h2>

<p>The <code>Compare</code> component (<code>Components/Shared/Compare.razor</code>) accepts two render
    fragments, <code>Plain</code> and <code>Bootstrapped</code>. The Bootstrap vs Plain page is built entirely from it.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;Compare Title="Panels"&gt;
    &lt;Plain&gt;Left content&lt;/Plain&gt;
    &lt;Bootstrapped&gt;Right content&lt;/Bootstrapped&gt;
&lt;/Compare&gt;</code></pre>

<Compare Title="Two panels from one component">
    <Plain>This is the <code>Plain</code> fragment.</Plain>
    <Bootstrapped>This is the <code>Bootstrapped</code> fragment.</Bootstrapped>
</Compare>

<h2>5. Templated components</h2>

<p>The <code>FancyList</code> component takes a list and a per-item template (<code>RenderFragment&lt;string&gt;</code>).
    The parent decides how each item looks using the <code>context</code> variable.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;FancyList Items="fruits"&gt;
    &lt;ItemTemplate context="fruit"&gt;
        &lt;span class="fw-bold"&gt;@@fruit&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/ItemTemplate&gt;
&lt;/FancyList&gt;</code></pre>

<FancyList Items="fruits">
    <ItemTemplate context="fruit">
        <span class="badge text-bg-success me-1">@fruit</span>
    </ItemTemplate>
</FancyList>

<h2>6. EditForm: validation with DataAnnotations</h2>

<pre><code>&lt;EditForm Model="form" OnValidSubmit="HandleValidSubmit"&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator/&gt;
    &lt;ValidationSummary/&gt;
    &lt;InputText @@bind-Value="form.Name"/&gt;
    &lt;ValidationMessage For="() =&gt; form.Name"/&gt;
&lt;/EditForm&gt;</code></pre>

<EditForm Model="form" FormName="demo-form" OnValidSubmit="HandleValidSubmit">
    <DataAnnotationsValidator/>
    <ValidationSummary/>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label class="form-label">Name</label>
        <InputText class="form-control" @bind-Value="form.Name"/>
        <ValidationMessage For="() => form.Name" class="text-danger"/>
    </div>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label class="form-label">Age</label>
        <InputNumber class="form-control" @bind-Value="form.Age"/>
        <ValidationMessage For="() => form.Age" class="text-danger"/>
    </div>

    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Submit</button>
</EditForm>

@if (submitted is not null)
{
    <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">@submitted</div>
}

<h2>7. Virtualize: rendering big lists cheaply</h2>

<p>Only the rows that fit on screen are actually created in the DOM. Scroll this list (500 rows).</p>

<p class="text-muted">Note: during the very first (static) page load the list looks empty, because the list is
    measured by JavaScript once the interactive circuit connects, then rows appear.</p>

<div style="height: 180px; overflow-y: auto;" class="border rounded p-2">
    <Virtualize Items="bigList" Context="item" ItemSize="36">
        <div class="border-bottom py-1">@item</div>
    </Virtualize>
</div>

@code {
    private PersonForm form = new();
    private string? submitted;
    private readonly List<string> fruits = new() { "Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Date", "Elderberry" };
    private readonly string[] bigList = Enumerable.Range(1, 500).Select(i => $"Row number {i}").ToArray();

    private void HandleValidSubmit()
    {
        submitted = $"Saved: {form.Name}, age {form.Age}.";
    }

    private class PersonForm
    {
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Name is required.")]
        public string Name { get; set; } = "";

        [Range(1, 120, ErrorMessage = "Age must be between 1 and 120.")]
        public int Age { get; set; } = 30;
    }
}
